{"id":"RQ1-A","finding":"Collaborative UAV inference spans multi-tier UAV-edge-cloud partitioning in R0001 and hierarchical swarm-native execution in R0014 that partitions the model among UAV clusters and data among UAVs inside clusters.","evidence":[["R0001",2,"Section II.A Inference Partitioning","There are five situations: 1. The inference service only executes on UAV."],["R0014",4,"Section III, Fig. 3","The model is partitioned among UAV clusters and the data is partitioned among UAVs inside clusters."]],"appraisal":[],"quantitative":[]}
{"id":"RQ1-B","finding":"Swarm-native collaborative inference divides into decentralized layer-wise partitioning with joint partner and model partition point selection in R0092 versus distributed Mixture-of-Experts deployment in R0118 where each UAV hosts a single dedicated DNN expert model.","evidence":[["R0092",6,"Section IV.A Overview and Design Rationale","DRL-based joint decision on partner selection and model partition point"],["R0118",4,"Section III.A UAV Swarm Model","Each UAV hosts a single dedicated DNN expert model of type"]],"appraisal":[],"quantitative":[]}
{"id":"RQ2-A","finding":"Mixed continuous-discrete UAV inference formulations are addressed by decomposing fixed-hover deployment and model partitioning into two coupled subproblems in R0006 or by decoupling 3D continuous movement, discrete video frame selection, and computation offloading into independent hybrid-action subspaces in R0064.","evidence":[["R0006",3,"Section III.A System Model","The UAVs hover at fixed positions to simultaneously collect data from their coverage areas"],["R0006",4,"Section IV Solution","decompose the original formulation into two coupled subproblems: model partitioning and UAV deployment"],["R0064",6,"Section 3.5 Problem formulation","the flying trajectory control of UAVs is continuous, and the offloading decision and the selection of video frame number are discrete"],["R0064",7,"Section 4.2 MAPPO-HA algorithm","3D continuous movement, discrete video frame selection, and computation offloading actions into three independent action subspaces"]],"appraisal":[],"quantitative":[]}
{"id":"RQ2-B","finding":"Non-DRL orchestration spans distributed single-server auction offloading maximizing global system utility in R0058 and alternating bit-allocation and threshold-update optimization in R0080 that decides whether only features or raw data are transmitted to the cloud.","evidence":[["R0058",11,"Section 4.2 Offloading problem modeling and bidding objectives","Each task can be offloaded to at most one server. The global optimization objective is"],["R0080",2,"Section II.A System Model","indicates that only features are uploaded; otherwise, raw data are transmitted to the cloud"],["R0080",5,"Section III.C Algorithm 1","Threshold update"]],"appraisal":[],"quantitative":[]}
{"id":"RQ3-A","finding":"Physical UAV testbed realism remains partial because R0014 runs outdoor experiments on 5 real-world UAVs without characterizing flight mobility or channels, while R0137 deploys offline-trained weights onto a physical multi-UAV testbed trained under a probabilistic path-loss model.","evidence":[["R0014",10,"Section VIII.A, Fig. 12","the outdoor experiments are conducted on 5 real-world UAVs"],["R0137",10,"Section VI.A Experimental Setup","extracted the trained model weights and deployed them onto our physical multi-UAV testbed"],["R0137",11,"Section VI.A Experimental Setup","Our communication model is built upon a probabilistic path-loss model"]],"appraisal":[["R0014","D07","PARTIAL"],["R0137","D07","PARTIAL"]],"quantitative":[]}
{"id":"RQ3-B","finding":"Comparator parity is partially bounded when R0118 evaluates MobileNetV3-Small MoE experts against a ResNet-50 layer-wise baseline under the same centralized online planner and R0125 scales workers with 4 threads allocated per worker.","evidence":[["R0118",9,"Section VI.B Evaluation Results","both NetMoE and the layer-wise baseline driven by the same centralized online planner"],["R0118",9,"Section VI.B Evaluation Results","Each MoE expert is taken to be of MobileNetV3-Small scale"],["R0125",3,"Section IV.B Distributed Inference Scalability","throughput scaling with increasing worker count, where each worker is allocated 4 threads"]],"appraisal":[["R0118","D04","PARTIAL"],["R0125","D04","PARTIAL"]],"quantitative":[]}
{"id":"RQ4-A","finding":"Simulated fault resilience mechanisms range from predictive and recovery migration under random UAV failures with a probability of 0.0001 per time-slot in R0002 to an initiator recovery state machine for mid-execution link interruption in R0092.","evidence":[["R0002",7,"Section VII.A Experimental Settings","random UAV failures are introduced in each time-slot with a probability of 0.0001"],["R0092",4,"Section III.C Link interruption and recovery","the initiator enters a recovery state machine"]],"appraisal":[],"quantitative":[]}
{"id":"RQ4-B","finding":"Prototype resilience evaluations cover benchtop container recovery that continued processing from the previous checkpoint in R0038 and hardware-in-the-loop UAV tracking handover tested on coastal shark footage in R0292.","evidence":[["R0038",5,"Section V.B.2 System Robustness","no data was lost, and the resumed container continued processing from the previous checkpoint"],["R0292",3,"Section 3.2 Dataset","footage capturing a shark swimming in shallow coastal waters"],["R0292",5,"Section 5.2 UAV Data Handover","The onboard system tests were performed in a hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) environment"]],"appraisal":[],"quantitative":[]}
{"id":"RQ5-A","finding":"At 100 Mbps with ResNet-18 on edge and ResNet-101 on cloud over three independent seeds with 95% Student-t confidence intervals, R0080 reports higher mean Top-1 accuracy and slightly higher mean latency for Ours than GSUP without formal significance inference.","evidence":[["R0080",5,"Section IV.A Experimental Setup and Table I","Each experimental setting was repeated three times with independent seeds. The reported results are presented as the mean with 95% Student-t confidence intervals."],["R0080",6,"Section IV.B Experiment Results","a bandwidth of 100 Mbps. First, the accuracy of ours is higher than that of the other three baseline methods"]],"appraisal":[],"quantitative":[["Top-1 Accuracy",90.4,0.5,89.5,0.5,"%","ResNet-18/101 Ours vs GSUP at 100 Mbps; mean and 95% Student-t CI half-width over 3 independent seeds"],["Latency",120,3,117,3,"ms","ResNet-18/101 Ours vs GSUP at 100 Mbps; mean and 95% Student-t CI half-width over 3 independent seeds"]]}
{"id":"RQ5-B","finding":"In the R0092 two-UAV simulator with shared Pareto Top-K and MiniBatch substrates and a strict all-arrived-tasks denominator over 30 common-seed episodes, VGG-16 DDCI reaches 71.6% (SD 3.4%) deadline satisfaction versus 64.4% (SD 4.3%) for Comm-Min, confirming the 4.3% SD erratum over the previously locked 3.6%.","evidence":[["R0092",13,"Table V, VGG-16 Deadline Sat. (%)","SATISFACTION RATES USE THE STRICT ALL-ARRIVED-TASKS DENOMINATOR"],["R0092",10,"Section V.B Baselines and Metrics","All collaborative baselines share the same Pareto Top-K pool and MiniBatch pipeline as DDCI"]],"appraisal":[["R0092","D04","YES"]],"quantitative":[["Deadline Satisfaction",71.6,3.4,64.4,4.3,"%","VGG-16 DDCI vs Comm-Min in two-UAV simulator, strict all-arrived denominator; mean and SD over 30 common-seed episodes (erratum: 4.3 SD supersedes prior 3.6)"]]}
{"id":"SUMMARY","claim_count":10,"tools_used":["client:list_dir","client:find_by_name","client:grep_search","client:view_file"],"workers_used":false,"limitations":["RQ1: Verified scope is limited to four named architectures (R0001, R0014, R0092, R0118) and excludes R0081, R0082, R0132 awaiting classification and unretrieved R0116.","RQ2: Verified scope covers four named formulations (R0006, R0058, R0064, R0080) without cross-study hyperparameter or runtime pooling.","RQ3: Testbed and baseline evidence in R0014, R0137, R0118, and R0125 carries PARTIAL D07 or D04 judgments due to uncharacterized flight/channel dynamics or unequal model/compute complexity.","RQ4: Resilience evidence in R0002, R0092, R0038, and R0292 relies on simulated failures, benchtop Docker-Redis tests, or HIL handover rather than live multi-UAV flight fault injection.","RQ5: Quantitative tuples are bounded to R0080 (3 seeds, 95% Student-t CI, no significance inference) and R0092 (30 common-seed episodes, SD, confirming the 4.3% Comm-Min SD erratum) without cross-study pooling."],"unresolved":[]}
